Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:50:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Fixed: Stop sending Korean text again after switching between
 Korean and English
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Ported from devshell-app 2d87b27d.

The input connection sent the text an IME committed to the terminal and
cleared it, without telling the IME. IMEs keep their own idea of the
text before the cursor, and Korean IMEs compose the word before it
again, e.g. after switching to English and back: the word could not be
replaced in the cleared text, so all of it was sent to the terminal a
second time, like 안녕안녕하 for 안녕하.

The text the IME committed is now kept in the input connection, like
the line it would edit in a text field, and each change is sent as the
backspaces and code points that turn the text sent so far into it.
Deleting more than the IME committed sends backspaces for the rest.

Keys sent to the terminal without the IME (letters typed in English,
Enter, arrows, extra keys) first send the text being composed and then
start input again with an empty text, since the text before the cursor
then no longer is the IME's. So does a new line committed by the IME,
and switching sessions after the IME committed text.
